ns is at most k * n (since each sunny line can intersect at most n columns). But we need to allocate them to columns.\n\nBut the key is that for each column c, the number of points covered by sunny lines in that column is at most k (since each sunny line can cover at most one point in that column). So the number of points left to be covered by vertical lines in column c is at least max(0, h_c - k). So we need at least sum_{c=1..n} max(0, h_c - k) vertical lines. But we have total n lines, of which k are sunny, so we have n - k non-sunny lines. Among non-sunny lines, only vertical lines can cover more than one point per column. Horizontal or diagonal lines can cover at most one point per column, like sunny lines. So to minimize the number of non-sunny lines needed, we would want them to be vertical lines covering as many points as possible. So we need at least sum_{c=1..n} ceil((h_c - k)/? Actually each vertical line can cover all points in a column, so one vertical line per column can cover all points in that column. So we need at least the number of columns where h_c > k, because if h_c ≤ k, we could cover all points in that column using sunny lines (one per point). But we have only k sunny lines total, not per column. So we cannot allocate k sunny lines to each column. So we need to consider distribution.\n\nBetter approach: Let's think of covering each column c. Suppose we have k sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point in column c. So the maximum number of points in column c that can be covered by sunny lines is at most k (if each sunny line covers a distinct point in that column). So the remaining points in column c must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since vertical lines can cover all remaining points in a column, we need at most one vertical line per column where h_c > k. So the number of vertical lines needed is at least the number of columns c such that h_c > k. Because if h_c > k, we cannot cover all points in that column using sunny lines 