lines left for the rest of the columns. But we also have at most 2 sunny lines. The rest of the columns have many points, and we need to cover them. The solution's argument might be that we cannot allocate enough lines to cover all points. But we need to verify.\n\nLet's try to formalize: The set of points is triangular. The columns are x=1,...,n. Column x=i has points (i,b) for b=1,...,n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points.\n\nWe have n lines total, k sunny, n-k non-sunny. Non-sunny lines are vertical (x=constant), horizontal (y=constant), or slope -1 (x+y=constant). Let's denote the number of vertical lines as v, horizontal lines as h, slope -1 lines as d. So v+h+d = n-k.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Each vertical line x=i covers all points in column i. Each horizontal line y=j covers all points in row j (i.e., points (i,j) for i from 1 to n+1-j). Each slope -1 line x+y=c covers all points with i+j=c, i.e., points on a diagonal.\n\nSunny lines can be any other lines, but they can intersect each column at at most one point (since they are not vertical). Similarly, they intersect each row at at most one point (since not horizontal), and each diagonal at at most one point (since not slope -1). So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, per row, per diagonal.\n\nThus the covering problem is akin to a combinatorial covering problem.\n\nWe need to find all possible k such that there exists a covering with n lines, exactly k sunny.\n\nWe have found constructions for k=0,1,3. We need to verify that k=2 is impossible. Also need to verify that no other k (like k≥4) is possible. The solution says k can only be 0,1,3. So we need to verify that k≥4 is impossible. Let's test k=4 for n=5: need 4 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line. Could we cover all points with 4 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line? Possibly? Let's try to see if we can cover all points with 5 lines, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line could be vertical x=1 covering column 1. Then we need to cover c